COMPATIBILITY OF MATHS FONTS
The LaTeXbook says that ${\bf ab}$ produces two bold letters, and similarly
\it \sf \rm \tt \sl.  For compatibility this has to stay - INDEFINITELY.
(see below).

However oldlfont.sty is NOT reverse-compatible, because with the AMS symbols
(implemented by importing a file from amstex) work(ed) the other way:
${\Bbb AB}$, etc, produce one blackboard bold letter and one maths italic.
Again this has to stay.

WHY INDEFINITELY?
I like Frank's solution of \documentclass for new things and \documentstyle
for old.  However it does not relieve you of the responsibility of still
supporting ${\bf ab}$ for two bold letters, because of cut-and-paste.

I have old notes of calculations I did some time ago which I intend to
include in a future book.  I've already spent the time proof-reading the
subscripts in all of the formulae and see no reason why I should have to
do it again, even when the surrounding text is completely new.

